<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tinchebray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1106">1106</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tinchebray was fought on 28 September 1106 in south-western Normandy between Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, duke of Normandy. Henry won quickly and captured Robert, who spent the rest of his life in captivity. The victory gave Henry control of Normandy and reunited it with England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tinchebray.jpg" alt="Battle of Tinchebray" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohan Master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tinchebray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later dukes fought for the duchy itself: Henry I of England defeated his brother Robert Curthose at the <b>Battle of Tinchebray</b> in 1106 and Louis VI of France at the Battle of Brémule in 1119.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
